# Load the Course Dataset Into Kibana

The companion to the setup video. This is a one-time thing. Do it once, and every lab in the course runs against the exact same logs you see here, so your answers can actually be checked against everyone else's.

## 📺 Watch the Video

🎥 [Coming soon — link will be added once the video publishes]

## 🎯 What You'll Walk Away With

- The course dataset loaded into your own Kibana, no Logstash or Filebeat required
- A data view named `soc-lab` that every lab points at
- Knowing why your time picker has to cover the data, or you'll see nothing

---

## Step 1: Get the file

Download the dataset from this repo:

[soc-lab-dataset.ndjson](./soc-lab-dataset.ndjson)

It's a small NDJSON file, 500 log records. Save it somewhere you can find it.

## Step 2: Open the file uploader

In Kibana, open the menu (top left) and go to **Machine Learning → Data Visualizer**, then click **Upload a file**. (Depending on your version you can also reach it from the **Add data** screen, "Upload a file.")

Drag the `.ndjson` file onto the page, or browse to it.

## Step 3: Review what Kibana found

Kibana reads the file and shows you a summary of the fields it detected. You don't need to change anything. Just confirm two things:

- The format is recognized as **NDJSON**
- The **time field** is set to `@timestamp`

If `@timestamp` is picked as the time field, you're good.

## Step 4: Import

Click **Import**. Choose the index name:

```
soc-lab
```

Leave the rest on defaults and let it run. When it finishes, make sure the option to **create a data view** is selected (also name it `soc-lab` if asked). That's the thing every lab will search.

## Step 5: Set your time range (this is the one that trips people up)

Open **Discover** and select the `soc-lab` data view. You will probably see zero results. That's expected. The logs in this file are dated, and your time picker defaults to the last 15 minutes.

Set the time picker to an **absolute range** that covers the data:

```
June 8, 2026 00:00  →  June 11, 2026 00:00
```

Now you should see logs. If you ever open a lab and the screen is empty, check the time picker first. It's almost always the time picker.

---

## 🧪 Verify You Loaded It Right

Run these two searches in Discover with the time range set as above. You should get the numbers below. If you do, you're set for every lab in the course.

- [ ] `event.dataset:system.auth` returns **192** documents
- [ ] `event.dataset:system.syslog` returns **308** documents

Total across both: 500.

